Pets sure can help out. I also find them extremely annoying when i
don't have a magic whistle (leash is okay), but i usually keep em
around because the benefit to me outweighs the annoyance most of the
time. But usually i'll be able to find a tripe ration soon enough and
keep it in my open inventory and that helps the pet stick around a bit.
"Pet Glue" is what Jove calls it, i believe. I use pets mainly for:

- Buffer in a corridor. Place your pet in between you and the baddies
and let them take care of business while you stand there safely. If you
have a stethoscope to monitor their health, even better.
- Taking out watchmen for their loot
- Robbing shops
- Determining B/U/C status of items so i can try them on without having
to lug it to an altar

All of those you probably know already. But i don't use pets for
finding traps, following in their footsteps or anything. I generally
don't use them for all my combat unless i'm running low on HP or i'm
weak for a particular area.

As for armor, with any starting character i put on the best AC i can
carry without being burdened. If that happens to be chain, then so be
it. At the beginning i'm not casting much with my wizard anyways
(throwing daggers), so it's usually not much of an issue. If i can find
a highly enchanted leather/studded leather chest piece then i'm one
happy camper. If my AC is starting to get comfortable (around -10 i
start relaxing a bit), then i can start thinking about replacing metal
items with more spell casting friendly ones.

Other advice (all of this is stolen from what the experienced players
say. credit to original authors):

Watch marvin's games. They're long, but rewarding. Throw daggers early
and often. Slow down. Don't worry about spell casting and ID'ing until
later. Run away. Elbereth. Know which monsters respect E.

